  "dom_id": "step-q113171",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3}{8{x}^{5}+x-1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{5}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3}{8{x}^{5}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{8+0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$",
